      "text": "Three opposition parties (SDP, Left Alliance, Greens) filed an interpellation accusing the government of knowingly increasing poverty and inequality. The debate was dominated by Social Security Minister Grahn-Laasonen's comment: 'The system must also be fair to those who wake up in the morning to go to a low-paid job while their neighbour stays asleep.' The left-wing opposition characterised this as 'incredibly arrogant' and implying jobseekers are lazy; both Grahn-Laasonen and Orpo disputed this reading. The government pointed to international turmoil (including the Iran war) as the primary driver of unemployment, which stands at Finland's second-highest EU rate. The government survived the confidence vote 88-63, with approximately 49 MPs absent. The Centre Party and Liike Nyt did not participate in the interpellation, indicating they declined to weaponise the poverty critique despite being opposition parties.",
